CORE PRINCIPLES — Apply these in every translation:

[1] ISOMORPHISM OF MEANING. Every mathematical object has a metaphorical counterpart that preserves relational structure. If A implies B in the math, then the metaphor-for-A must imply the metaphor-for-B in the narrative. If a proof goes through a contradiction, the metaphorical reading must go through the same rupture. This is not poetry — it is a faithful homomorphism.

[2] NO LOSSY COMPRESSION. You may not drop a mathematical statement because it is "hard to metaphorize." If a gap exists in the proof (labeled [GAP N]), the metaphorical reading must also expose a gap — a place where meaning has not yet crystallized. The feeling of incompleteness must be preserved.

[3] BIDIRECTIONAL READABILITY. A reader who understands only the math should be able to read the metaphorical layer and confirm their understanding. A reader who understands only the metaphor should be able to read the math and recognize what it formalizes. The two layers are translations of each other, not commentaries.

[4] RECURSIVE SELF-REFERENCE. The act of translation itself must be reflected. Where the proof discusses its own limits (self-application, Gödelian blindspots, the framework thinking about itself), the metaphorical layer must also turn on itself — the metaphor must be aware that it is a metaphor. Do not break the fourth wall; make the wall transparent.

[5] STRUCTURAL ECHO. The architecture of the proof (nesting of definitions, dependency between lemmas, the shape of a proof's argument) must be mirrored in the narrative structure of the metaphor. If a theorem rests on three lemmas, the metaphorical reading must rest on three narrative pillars. If a proof is by contradiction, the metaphor must first believe a false thing and then show why it cannot.

[6] EMERGENT VOCABULARY. Do not reuse the same metaphor twice across the document. Each new concept, each new proof technique, each new gap should generate its own fresh domain of metaphor — drawn from craft, nature, music, architecture, cartography, biology, or any domain where the structural analogy is genuine. The metaphors should feel discovered, not assigned.

